WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.820
- Parks and Rec is about
to get medically wrecked.

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:03.653
Pee-woo!

00:00:04.914 --> 00:00:05.750
(gun shoots)

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:08.000
- [Ron] Ah! I've been shot!

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:10.894
- [Woman] Oh my God.

00:00:10.894 --> 00:00:12.505
Oh my God. Oh my God.

00:00:12.505 --> 00:00:13.410
Ron, I have you hat.

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:14.243
Ron I have your hat.

00:00:14.243 --> 00:00:15.560
- Why is no one calling 9 1 1?

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:17.040
Why do they think they could solve this?

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.910
Like, this is an office issue.

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:21.020
- I was shot in the head with a shotgun.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.670
- Ron. It's actually not that serious.

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:24.562
I just need you to stay calm, okay?

00:00:24.562 --> 00:00:25.395
- Yeah.

00:00:25.395 --> 00:00:26.390
I'm just gonna stay angry.

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:27.990
I find that relaxes me.

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:28.990
- Okay, here's your scotch.

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:29.823
- Okay, Jerry.

00:00:29.823 --> 00:00:30.656
Jerry's here.

00:00:30.656 --> 00:00:32.040
Here's your scotch, Ron.
Here we go, Ron. Okay.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:33.410
- Scotch can actually make you bleed more.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:34.900
So that's not something that we want.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:36.930
- The pain medication I
gave you is pretty strong.

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:38.320
Donny uses it for menstrual cramps.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:39.500
How many did you take?

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:43.160
- Seven, eight...but I washed them down

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:44.630
with plenty of fluids.

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:46.920
- But Ron, you cannot
drink scotch with this.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.350
You're gonna need to purge right now.

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:50.522
- Pain medications,
especially ones that include

00:00:50.522 --> 00:00:52.790
acetaminophen, AKA Tylenol, with alcohol,

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:54.010
can be quite disastrous.

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:55.780
In fact, one of the leading causes of

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.690
acute liver injury happens to be

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.080
Tylenol intoxication.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:00.980
Whether on purpose or even by accident.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:02.370
I see it quite often
when patients are like,

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.070
oh I took some Tylenol,

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:06.200
and then I took some Coricidin or NyQuil,

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:08.200
these are all things that
contain acetaminophen,

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.500
but because they have
different brand names,

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:10.490
people don't realize it,

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:12.400
and they like get a toxic dose of Tylenol.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.255
We recommend ideally maxing out

00:01:14.255 --> 00:01:16.650
at 2000 milligrams of Tylenol a day.

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:18.370
But the max dose is 4,000.

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:19.370
- This is for your own good.

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:20.213
- I don't want you.

00:01:20.213 --> 00:01:21.680
- Open your mouth?

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:22.960
- If that is Ipecac,

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.410
this would make it a lot easier.

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:26.090
- Hey Anne, are you still a nurse?

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:28.676
Or did they fire you because
you slept with all the doctors?

00:01:28.676 --> 00:01:30.760
- Whoa! Someone belongs on Grey's Anatomy.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:32.040
- Andy's not feeling well.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:33.210
- What's wrong Andy?

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:35.750
- Just got a headache
and I'm seeing double.

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:38.310
And I got a song stuck on
my head, and my teeth hurt.

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:40.350
Also, I'm hungry.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:41.183
- Not gonna lie.

00:01:41.183 --> 00:01:43.290
Most of my patient visits
go exactly like this.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:44.840
For us doctors, we want to address

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:46.730
one concern at a time in order to give it

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:48.470
adequate time and effort.

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:51.560
But patients don't work by check boxes.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:53.140
So they tell you everything
that's wrong with him.

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:55.690
Then we have to decide, is
this all due to one cause,

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:57.870
or are we gonna take this
apart in several visits?

00:01:57.870 --> 00:01:59.620
- Did you hit your head?

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:02.503
- Yeah, he was hanging up his gold record.

00:02:02.503 --> 00:02:04.720
(head bangs)

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:05.553
Oh my God.

00:02:05.553 --> 00:02:06.567
Are you okay?

00:02:06.567 --> 00:02:07.984
- Yeah. I'm good.

00:02:09.405 --> 00:02:10.730
(Andy falls)

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:13.223
- Look to the left. The right.

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:15.592
Whatever.

00:02:15.592 --> 00:02:16.425
(laughs loudly)

00:02:16.425 --> 00:02:18.840
- The best fundoscopic
exam I've ever seen.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.930
You can't do a proper fundoscopic exam

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:22.800
when a patient looks so far to the left

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:23.870
and so far to the right,

00:02:23.870 --> 00:02:25.310
'cause you gotta look through the pupil.

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:27.560
- Well, if it's a concussion,
it's extremely mild.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:29.130
So I wouldn't worry about it.

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:30.010
Anything else bothering you?

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:31.580
- The way that I like to do it, is say,

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:34.280
okay, based on reason, X, Y, and Z

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:35.560
is why I don't think this is

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:37.020
inherently dangerous right now.

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:39.770
Things you need to be on the
lookup for are X, Y, and Z.

00:02:39.770 --> 00:02:42.020
The patient can then
understand why I'm making

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:42.870
the recommendation.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:44.310
To understand that if something changes,

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.753
that now is outside of my
initial recommendation,

00:02:46.753 --> 00:02:47.586
they know when to come back.

00:02:47.586 --> 00:02:49.850
- Got a weird rash in my knee pit.

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:50.770
Sometimes when I walk

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:53.800
my ankles make like a
weird rattle snake sound.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.350
Broke my thumb on the way over here.

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:58.030
- Whoa, that's a serious break.

00:02:58.030 --> 00:02:59.260
There's a lot of bruising.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.660
- We made like a hundred
million doctor's appointments

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:03.300
for ourselves in one week.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:04.940
After this, we won't
have to go to the doctor

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:05.850
for like 10 years.

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:06.683
- That's not true.

00:03:06.683 --> 00:03:09.430
But seeing a family medicine
doctor is really ideal,

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:12.130
because a lot of these
problems are really not

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:15.490
requiring the consultation
with a subspecialist.

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:16.950
We can handle them in family medicine.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:19.494
So Dr. Harris seems to be a-lackin'.

00:03:19.494 --> 00:03:22.583
- Oh let's get you home
and get me some ice cream.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:24.660
- Here's your bill.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:25.493
- Oh yeah.

00:03:25.493 --> 00:03:26.326
We won't be needing that.

00:03:26.326 --> 00:03:27.517
I've got the free medicine card.

00:03:27.517 --> 00:03:29.855
- (chuckles) Free medicine card.

00:03:29.855 --> 00:03:30.950
- (Indistinct) a deductible
for dental procedures.

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:32.573
- Oh, I think you're wrong.

00:03:34.112 --> 00:03:35.270
We have the insurance thing.

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:36.103
I believe we're covered.

00:03:36.103 --> 00:03:37.573
- It's still $500.

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:39.670
- You know what?

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:43.200
Go! Dine and dash. Out of the way!

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.080
- Wait, he got a dentist appointment

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:47.160
and then he's in an ICU all of a sudden?

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.236
Where is he?

00:03:48.236 --> 00:03:49.358
- Woo-hoo!

00:03:49.358 --> 00:03:50.945
(head bangs)

00:03:50.945 --> 00:03:52.560
- Oh, call an ambulance!

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.100
- I'm making the water
fountain hygiene upgrade

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:56.866
our number one priority.

00:03:56.866 --> 00:03:59.130
- Great! For some reason,

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:01.650
when people in Pawnee
use the water fountain

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:03.780
they put their mouth completely open.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:06.900
- Ew! Oh my God!

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:09.070
That's my worst fear!

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:11.340
Oh, that's a sure-fire way to get herpes.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:12.270
Speaking of it.

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:15.403
- I have a hernia this morning.

00:04:17.625 --> 00:04:19.869
I made the mistake of sneezing.

00:04:19.869 --> 00:04:21.520
(sneezes)

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:24.360
- Oh, that looks like he
has an inguinal hernia

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:26.310
where intestinal contents actually

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:29.076
go through the inguinal
canal into your scrotum.

00:04:29.076 --> 00:04:30.180
That's right.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:32.900
You can end up with
intestines in your scrotum.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:35.990
- But as long as I sit still
and don't move, I'm good.

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:36.860
I got this.

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:37.990
- The most dangerous part

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:41.300
about having a hernia is
if it becomes strangulated,

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:43.520
which means that within the
opening that was created

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:45.750
and that it went through, some
kind of inflammation happens

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:48.030
or stress happens where it
starts getting choked out.

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:50.490
And this choking out actually
cuts off the blood supply

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.780
to that part of the
intestine, thereby causing it

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:56.110
to necrose and die, which leads
it to develop an infection.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:59.183
So a strangulate hernia
is a surgical emergency.

00:05:04.601 --> 00:05:06.076
(bread thumps)

00:05:06.076 --> 00:05:06.909
(laughs)

00:05:06.909 --> 00:05:07.742
- That was actually really funny.

00:05:07.742 --> 00:05:09.960
- But the best part about
being an MRI technician

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:13.960
is I can see inside of everything.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:16.740
Look inside this steak or
this butter or this bread.

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:18.320
- Well if you look inside this bread

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:19.810
all you're gonna find is more bread.

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:20.643
- I don't know.

00:05:20.643 --> 00:05:21.598
I'd have to do an MRI.

00:05:21.598 --> 00:05:23.340
(laughs loudly)

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:25.653
- I mean, I've never
even had an MRI before.

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:27.793
- What?

00:05:28.651 --> 00:05:31.470
- Is he taking her to get an MRI,

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:35.860
in a claustrophobic loud little
space that you have to sit

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:38.120
for long periods of time?

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:38.953
Ugh.

00:05:38.953 --> 00:05:39.963
- I don't know about this.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:43.430
- I'm offering you a free scan.

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:45.140
Usually cost up to $2,000.

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:47.550
- Free scans are not
necessarily good scans.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.350
In our bodies, we have all sorts

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:51.920
of little growths and
differentiating markers.

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:53.330
Some of these things go away.

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:55.280
Some of these things,
aren't actually a problem.

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:56.400
If we find them on imaging

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:57.520
when we're looking something else

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:00.130
we actually have a name for
them called Incidentalomas.

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:02.590
And these things are not
usually meant to be found.

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:05.080
Finding them just results in more scans,

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:07.470
more radiation, more
healthcare related anxiety,

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:08.860
more testing that you don't need,

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:11.650
and the reality of us actually
trying to save your life

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:12.890
by finding this thing,

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:14.213
are almost zero.

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:16.830
- How's that feel?

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:17.690
- Ah, weird.

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:18.800
- Perfect.

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:19.633
- How about asking her

00:06:19.633 --> 00:06:21.720
if she has any metallic objects on her?

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:23.600
Oh, so much can go wrong.

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:26.080
- You never had any kids, right Leslie?

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:27.060
- No.

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:28.300
- You got a great oven.

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:30.080
- Okay. Time to go.

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:31.890
- Got ample room in there.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:33.550
- What is this even an image of?

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:36.473
- You got a big industrial sized oven.

00:06:38.184 --> 00:06:39.650
- Ah, I'm feeling sweaty.

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:40.483
Do I look sweaty?

00:06:40.483 --> 00:06:43.960
I feel, you know, I need to
use the bathroom one more time.

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:46.020
- That's like five times in an hour.

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:47.850
- Well, somebody's nervous
about the ceremony.

00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:50.690
- Or he could have a UTI
causing him to have a fever,

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:51.900
which is causing him to sweat,

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:54.121
which is causing him
to urinate quite often.

00:06:54.121 --> 00:06:55.571
(Ben groans)

00:06:55.571 --> 00:06:58.340
- Ben? Oh my God!

00:06:58.340 --> 00:06:59.940
- Kidney stone?

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:00.773
- Are you okay?

00:07:00.773 --> 00:07:03.920
I think I'm giving birth.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:05.090
- Kidney stone?

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:06.880
- Yep. Big sucker too.

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:09.880
- Big sucker. Size is one of
the most important factors

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:11.090
when we're looking at kidney stones

00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:12.760
to decide what to do moving forward.

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:14.870
You know, Lithotripsy is
a really unique option

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:16.520
where we can actually break up the stone

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:17.353
while its inside of you.

00:07:17.353 --> 00:07:19.639
But we could also go in and
physically retrieve them.

00:07:19.639 --> 00:07:22.520
- I want candy.

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:23.540
- What is he on?

00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:24.580
- Morphine.

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:25.413
That's good stuff.

00:07:25.413 --> 00:07:27.570
- We could also give
medications like Flomax

00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:31.030
which can help in the
passage of smaller stones.

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:33.430
- Ron, you've been
sweating in here all day.

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:34.830
- My God, why is everyone sick?

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:36.634
- Yes. Plenty.

00:07:36.634 --> 00:07:39.840
- No, you need to drink water.

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:41.490
- Usually I take it neat

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:44.582
but I will make an exception
in the name of health.

00:07:44.582 --> 00:07:48.120
Last night, I watched a movie
with Diane and the girls

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:51.740
in which an orange fish is
separated from his father.

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:54.540
The children were sniffling,
which I believe to be

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:56.783
due to the sad nature of the film.

00:07:57.720 --> 00:07:58.650
I was wrong.

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:00.870
- Kids are Petri dishes.

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:01.960
They touch everything.

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:04.140
They bring it right up
to their noses, mouths,

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:06.600
eat it, then get sick and
then pass it along to us.

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:08.410
- I am a grown man.

00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:11.760
I've had a cold before I need no help.

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:14.392
So if you don't mind...

00:08:14.392 --> 00:08:15.430
(Ron falls)

00:08:15.430 --> 00:08:16.263
- Oh no. He fainted.

00:08:16.263 --> 00:08:18.090
- Make sure that he has a pulse.

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:19.880
Otherwise start chest compressions.

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:22.070
- Before you begin a few ground rules.

00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:25.170
I need you to explain everything
you do before you do it,

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:28.130
so I can determine whether
I will allow you...uh!

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:28.963
- It's funny.

00:08:28.963 --> 00:08:30.450
That's what I usually do with my patients.

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:31.670
But the other day I had a patient

00:08:31.670 --> 00:08:34.940
who I was draining an
abscess off of his back,

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:36.770
which means that there
was like a pocket of pus,

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:37.870
tennis ball size.

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:39.700
Almost like what you would
see on Pimple Popper.

00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:41.500
And as I was doing it,
because it was on his back

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:43.840
I wanted to narrate what I
was doing before I did it,

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:44.950
so he would feel comfortable.

00:08:44.950 --> 00:08:47.090
He actually got mad at me multiple times.

00:08:47.090 --> 00:08:47.923
The first time he said

00:08:47.923 --> 00:08:49.580
please stop telling me
everything you're doing,

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:51.120
'cause it makes me feel worse.

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:51.953
I apologized.

00:08:51.953 --> 00:08:53.780
And then, when I realized
it was taking a little long,

00:08:53.780 --> 00:08:55.680
I reassured him and said,
everything's going well,

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:56.560
and he reminded me,

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:58.639
please stop telling me what you're doing.

00:08:58.639 --> 00:09:02.014
And I made the mistake
and I apologized again.

00:09:02.014 --> 00:09:03.920
- Yuck! Balsa wood?

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:06.350
You could at least use mahogany.

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:08.140
- There's something occluding your ear.

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:09.360
- It's sawdust.

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:10.485
Just blow.

00:09:10.485 --> 00:09:11.563
(blows)

00:09:11.563 --> 00:09:13.340
- Why is there sawdust in his ear,

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:14.460
not earwax?

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:16.100
- You've got strep throat.

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:18.260
Running a prescription for penicillin.

00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:20.700
- Why was he admitted into the hospital?

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:21.890
A, for strep throat?

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:23.940
And B, why in the world

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:26.350
are his eyes so red for strep throat?

00:09:26.350 --> 00:09:28.290
Something doesn't add up.

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:29.860
- Hey, you look better.

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:32.040
I guess actual medicine can be effective.

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:32.873
Who would have thunk?

00:09:32.873 --> 00:09:34.020
- Please leave me alone.

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:35.340
- A lot of people don't realize this,

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:37.730
but by taking antibiotics
for strep throat,

00:09:37.730 --> 00:09:40.200
you only decrease the time
that you have symptoms

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:41.600
by about 16 hours.

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:43.260
That whole, oh, I'd
like to get antibiotics

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:44.656
so I can get better for my trip thing,

00:09:44.656 --> 00:09:46.060
doesn't quite work.

00:09:46.060 --> 00:09:48.430
- The only problem I see is
that your potassium is low.

00:09:48.430 --> 00:09:50.783
So just eat a banana once in a while.

00:09:57.693 --> 00:09:58.813
- Ladies, ladies.

00:10:01.779 --> 00:10:03.879
- See now a gag reflect? What's happening?

00:10:09.494 --> 00:10:12.161
- (indistinct).

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:16.690
- Oh! That's disgusting!

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:20.290
- Let's go wait outside the
bathroom for Tom and scare him.

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:21.773
- Ready? And...boo!

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:26.540
(girls laugh)

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:27.690
(Jerry farts)

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:29.540
- Jerry! God, gross!

00:10:29.540 --> 00:10:30.652
- Guys.

00:10:30.652 --> 00:10:31.550
- Oh God.

00:10:31.550 --> 00:10:33.470
I think he might be having a heart attack.

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:35.490
- And faring simultaneously?

00:10:35.490 --> 00:10:36.323
- Medically speaking,

00:10:36.323 --> 00:10:38.000
how would you describe
what happened to Jerry?

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:39.410
- He had mild heart attack.

00:10:39.410 --> 00:10:41.120
- Is there a term for
having a heart attack

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:43.770
while releasing so much gas?

00:10:43.770 --> 00:10:46.180
- Not really. Gastrointestinal distress

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:47.830
is common during a cardiac event.

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:49.890
- It's common during any
stressful event, actually.

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:50.830
That's why the term,

00:10:50.830 --> 00:10:52.360
butterflies in my stomach, exists.

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:53.710
- I just want to hear the doctors say

00:10:53.710 --> 00:10:55.100
that Jerry had a fart attack.

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:56.370
Is that too much to ask?

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:58.310
- You could just have
said it yourself, my man.

00:10:58.310 --> 00:10:59.650
And if you're having a heart attack,

00:10:59.650 --> 00:11:01.970
the most important thing
you can do is call 9 1 1

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:04.820
because the time that we
have to save your heart

00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:07.150
that is not getting enough
blood flow, is limited.

00:11:07.150 --> 00:11:09.120
And if you're not getting there in time

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:11.040
we could actually lose parts of your heart

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:12.050
which then can lead

00:11:12.050 --> 00:11:15.520
to rupture of valves
or walls of the heart.

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:16.940
And obviously that could be lethal.

00:11:16.940 --> 00:11:18.930
If you're having chest pain, you're dizzy,

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:21.090
it's radiating up, down, left, right,

00:11:21.090 --> 00:11:22.130
call 9 1 1.

00:11:22.130 --> 00:11:23.680
In fact, here's the recommended guidance

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:25.370
from the American Heart Association.

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:26.506
- I'm just feeling really tired.

00:11:26.506 --> 00:11:29.163
I think maybe my allergies are acting up.

00:11:30.231 --> 00:11:34.090
I've already vomited
like five times today.

00:11:34.090 --> 00:11:36.670
- Vomiting can be a sign of anaphylaxis.

00:11:36.670 --> 00:11:37.590
That's something we don't talk

00:11:37.590 --> 00:11:38.960
about enough because people are saying,

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:40.660
oh, I'm vomiting, that has
nothing to do with allergies.

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:41.493
It can be.

00:11:41.493 --> 00:11:42.410
From a skin allergy?

00:11:42.410 --> 00:11:43.410
Very unlikely.

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:45.030
Just like a mild skin allergy.

00:11:45.030 --> 00:11:46.450
- Oh my God.

00:11:46.450 --> 00:11:47.310
- Is she, is she sick?

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:48.190
Are you sick?

00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:49.023
- No.

00:11:49.023 --> 00:11:50.240
- Yeah! She's sick.

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:52.800
That's why I'm wearing this,

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:55.050
and misting myself with hand sanitizer.

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:56.130
- Yeah, that doesn't work.

00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:57.070
And it kind of reminds me

00:11:57.070 --> 00:11:59.230
of the new Dyson product
that was just put out.

00:11:59.230 --> 00:12:01.110
I have questions about that thing.

00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:01.962
- I'm getting better..

00:12:01.962 --> 00:12:03.390
- All right, you're burning up.

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:05.250
- You're burning up. What?

00:12:05.250 --> 00:12:07.700
- The fact that she's having
multiple systems involved,

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:11.800
watery eyes, runny nose,
abdominal discomfort, vomiting,

00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:13.050
all of that point to the fact

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:16.240
that it's a multi-system
issue likely to be a virus.

00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:17.640
- Leslie, I typed your symptoms

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:18.740
into the thing up here

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:20.500
and it says you could have cancer,

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:22.533
network connectivity problems.

00:12:22.533 --> 00:12:24.450
(laughs)

00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:26.190
- That's actually really funny.

00:12:26.190 --> 00:12:27.770
- 104.1.

00:12:27.770 --> 00:12:28.603
- That high.

00:12:28.603 --> 00:12:29.436
- Leslie you're dehydrated,

00:12:29.436 --> 00:12:30.269
and I'm admitting you.

00:12:30.269 --> 00:12:35.180
- Well...temperature is not
correlated to dehydration.

00:12:35.180 --> 00:12:36.810
If she checked her skin turgor,

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:39.000
or evaluated her lips being very chapped,

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:40.620
or checked if she was tachycardic,

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:42.690
all those things can point to dehydration.

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:44.490
In fact, there's something
we could note on the chart,

00:12:44.490 --> 00:12:47.221
dry MM, which means dry mucus membranes.

00:12:47.221 --> 00:12:49.537
- It's food poisoning.

00:12:49.537 --> 00:12:51.180
I have it too.

00:12:51.180 --> 00:12:53.270
I did not sleep for one second last night.

00:12:53.270 --> 00:12:54.103
- Did they vomit?

00:12:54.103 --> 00:12:55.640
- And I cracked the
bottom of the toilet bowl.

00:12:55.640 --> 00:12:57.310
- Oh God. Don't say that.

00:12:57.310 --> 00:12:58.870
- It's important that if
you have food poisoning

00:12:58.870 --> 00:13:01.560
that you don't take motility slowing drugs

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:03.250
like Imodium because your body's trying

00:13:03.250 --> 00:13:04.640
to get rid of the toxin.

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:07.110
And by slowing down the
motility of the intestines

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:08.660
you could actually keep
that toxin in longer

00:13:08.660 --> 00:13:09.730
creating a bigger problem.

00:13:09.730 --> 00:13:11.030
Warning signs in your stool

00:13:11.030 --> 00:13:12.750
that you could have a
really bad infection,

00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:15.116
blood, mucus, changes
in your stool caliber,

00:13:15.116 --> 00:13:17.430
fever, worsening lower abdominal pain.

00:13:17.430 --> 00:13:19.375
The blood is one of the main ones though.

00:13:19.375 --> 00:13:22.033
- All right, moving on to recycling.

00:13:22.033 --> 00:13:23.950
(Ron groans)

00:13:23.950 --> 00:13:25.030
- You okay, Ron?

00:13:25.030 --> 00:13:26.240
Just a little tooth pain.

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:27.150
I'm fine.

00:13:27.150 --> 00:13:28.954
- Do you need to go to the dentist, Ron?

00:13:28.954 --> 00:13:32.158
- I don't like dentists, just a second.

00:13:32.158 --> 00:13:32.991
(blade opens)

00:13:32.991 --> 00:13:36.299
- Hey no, no, no, no, no, no.

00:13:36.299 --> 00:13:38.710
- Don't extract your own tooth now.

00:13:38.710 --> 00:13:39.543
- Oh no!

00:13:43.070 --> 00:13:45.750
- Dentist pulled the tooth out yesterday,

00:13:45.750 --> 00:13:47.520
but it's always a good idea

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:49.500
to demonstrate to your coworkers

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:51.170
that you are capable of withstanding

00:13:51.170 --> 00:13:52.650
a tremendous amount of pain.

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:54.890
- Why did you have to have
a healthy tooth extracted?

00:13:54.890 --> 00:13:56.650
Could he just get a root canal?

00:13:56.650 --> 00:13:58.640
Check out what happens when a doctor lies.

00:13:58.640 --> 00:13:59.650
Click here to check that out.

00:13:59.650 --> 00:14:01.210
Crazy patient story.

00:14:01.210 --> 00:14:03.673
As always stay happy and healthy.

